1. Introduction

   Prerequisites: Animation Software: Procreate
   ($): It's a little clunky to animate, not the best Layer Management
   Procreate Dreams: Also a little clunky to animate in, good for tweening, kind of a bit complicated.
   Rough Animator (5$): For animation I WOULD VERY MUCH RECOMMEND using
   this it's ony 5$ and is a lot easier to use for animation than
   procreate, especially because it has easy layers. You could almost do
   your animation in here, figure it out and then import it into
   procreate if you like the brush engine more in procreate. Blender
   (Free): Computer Only - great for animating 2D and CG. Vector based.
   TVPaint (300-500$ One time License Fee forever): Calarts and Gobelin
   and most personal or indipendent projects are done with TVPaint, it
   is french though and will take a few weeks to arrive. I wouldn't
   right away recommend it, but it's good to keep in mind for the future
   and to know about for students. Many Animation Studios in Japan use
   TVPaint. Toonboom Harmony (20+$ Month): This is another program I'm
   not recommending right away but this is the industry standard for
   most American shows Dropbox Account -> If you're unable to have a
   dropbox account please let me know but it saves time and maximizes
   efficiency :-) Dropbox offers cloud syncing and sharing, so if
   student posts work their it will already sync to my computer -10
   minutes to communicate what your project is Overview of objectives
   and goals of class

2. - Things to think about before class and that we will cover:

   - where do you want to focus your portfolio?
   - what are the objectives?
   - What is required for your project: Competition: High School
     Competition: Summer Camp Art Application: College
     Application: -[[https://calarts.edu/apply/bfa-character-animation#application]]

     - observational artwork
     - life drawings of human models
     - observational drawings from real life

     = other creative work

     - sequential image storytelling digital creative work other work
       nondigital
     - artist statement
     - video introduction
     - letters of recommendation
     - sketchbook video

   Animation terms used in class: Drawing - A drawing in animation is
   the singular drawing Frame - the frame is the number that the drawing
   is on so Drawing 7 is on frame 29 and should be moved to frame 45
   Exposure - the exposure is how long a drawing is held for and also
   the count number that the drawing is to be filmed on, this comes from
   exposure sheet when animators would animate and hand their work to be
   filmed they would hand an exposure sheet explaining how long each
   drawing was to be held and so on Dailies - is the name for looking
   through your work for review. we will have dailies to look at your
   work and adjust and give note. Animation is a lot of revision in the
   early stages, it's good to lay the foundation first before building
   on top so that the animation doesn't collapse when being built :-) .
   Arcs - arcs are the natural curved paths that masses move in Timing -
   the time you take in between each drawing

